# -*- mode: org -*-
#+TITLE: The queue for the stdcxx revival
#+STARTUP: overview

The queue, in priority order, top first. Each entry states the fact,
the intended fix, and the check that settles it. Finished entries
leave this file; git remembers them. The =analysis-= notes under
=doc/notes= carry the longer investigations.

* TODO Test-case structs: =size_t= fields, =NPOS= sentinel

=StringTestCase= (=tests/include/rw_strings.h=) and =ContainerTestCase=
(=tests/include/rw_containers.h=) hold positions and counts in =int=
fields, and every table spells "not applicable" as =-1=, which reaches
the library as =size_t= and lands as =npos=: the sentinel and =npos=
are the same number by design.

Fix, as one inert commit after the baseline: retype =off=, =size=,
=off2= and =size2= to =_RWSTD_SIZE_T= in both structs, and change the
sentinel to =NPOS= in those four positions across the 171 =TEST= macro
bodies and the rows that pass it; =val= and =bthrow= stay =int= with
their own =-1=.

Check: identical assertion counts for every string and container test
before and after.

* TODO =money_get='s value buffer has no bound

=money_get::_C_get= (=include/loc/_money_get.cc=) collects the digits
of the value in a 304-byte array on the stack and writes into it
without a check, the digits as they are read and the zeros
=frac_digits= pads them with; a monetary value of more characters
overruns the frame. A 20000-digit value read through
=istreambuf_iterator= dies of SIGSEGV in 11S; =num_get= had the same
array with a check that set =failbit= until its buffers moved to the
heap on demand.

Fix: the shape =num_get= has now, the array moved to a heap block
of twice the size when full, the exit test in a guard; the
group-size array beside it grows with it.

Check: a value of ten thousand digits reads to the end with =eofbit=
alone and the expected magnitude; the =22.locale.money.get= row
unchanged.

* TODO The wide half of UserCtype

=tests/src/ctype.cpp= implements =UserCtype<wchar_t>= and
=UserCtype<UserChar>= only in part: ten members are "FIXME: implement
this" stubs, and =make_char= (=tests/include/rw_char.h=) takes only a
=char=. =tests/self/0.ctype.cpp= carries the matching FIXME and breaks
out of its table at =UCHAR_MAX=, so the wide entries of its character
table have never been exercised on any platform.

Fix: finish the helper, give =make_char= an =int= overload, remove the
break.

Check: =0.ctype= exercises the whole table for all three character
types.

* TODO README section 5 and the configuration

The original README recommends setting =CPPOPTS= and =CXXOPTS= on the
make command line to change compiler options. The configuration
directory is insulated from those variables by design: =config.h= is a
function of the compiler, its version and the build type, and
anything the configuration must see goes in the =CONFIG= file. A
dialect option passed only on the command line produces a =config.h=
for one language and a library built in another.

Fix: document, not patch; the per-platform manual step the README
describes is a defect of the configuration to move away from, not a
behaviour to propagate. =README.md= already says so; the original
README stays as it was.

Check: none; this is prose.

* TODO std::locale MT-safety: what remains

The locale races are fixed; =doc/notes/analysis-locale-mt-race.md=
chapter 7 lists the fixes and their tests. ThreadSanitizer reports
six on a round of the locale MT tests: four in the test driver, one
in a test, and the global locale's =ginit= flag, which the sweep
entry below covers.

Open:
- The string reference count. 15D keeps one mutex per string body
  for 4.1.x binary compatibility (=_RWSTD_NO_STRING_ATOMIC_OPS=,
  =include/rw/_config.h:108=). =21.string.stdcxx-162= aborts on it in
  15D: the body is 64 bytes, the test allows 24. Decide: atomics now,
  or the mutex until the next minor version.
- =_RWSTD_NO_THREAD_SAFE_LOCALE=. =THREAD_SAFE_LOCALE.cpp=
  characterizes it; nothing reads it. Find the consumer it was meant
  for, or retire the test.

Check: the ThreadSanitizer build of 15D runs every =22.locale.*.mt=
test three times with no report from the library; the
=21.string.stdcxx-162= row follows the decision.

* TODO MT tests that provoke contention instead of waiting for it

The sixteen =22.locale.*.mt= tests run one thread per processor, up
to 32, for 10,000 to 200,000 iterations per thread, each threaded
section until its own 60-second soft timeout. After the first
iteration everything they touch is cached, so the rest measures the
library's locks in steady state: at 32 threads =22.locale.numpunct.mt=
keeps about one core busy for two minutes. On 32 processors the
harness's 1 GiB address-space limit (=etc/config/GNUmakefile.tst=,
2007, for build machines shared with other products) cannot hold 32
threads' 8 MiB stacks and 64 MiB =malloc= arenas, so the pool fails
to start, and a test that starts outruns the 60-second harness
timeout. The two tests written the other way,
=22.locale.use_facet.mt= and =22.locale.time.put.libc.mt=, find
their races in a second or two: a few threads parked on a barrier,
released together at the first use of a fresh object, round after
round.

The gated tests now number four (=22.locale.use_facet.mt=,
=22.locale.time.put.libc.mt=, =22.locale.id.mt=,
=22.locale.facet.mt=), and they overlap the old ones: the first use
of a facet, its data and its reference counts are exercised by both.
Each old test also covers several facets and operations at once, so a
failure names a test and not a defect.

Fix: rewrite the locale MT tests in that shape, or retire the ones
the gated tests make redundant, and split what remains finer, one
race or one facet operation per program, so that a row of the table
names what failed: 4 to 8 threads whatever the
processor count, released from a barrier, many short rounds each on
fresh state (a new locale or facet) so the first-use window opens
again, a small thread stack; steady-state races are left to the
ThreadSanitizer build, which needs few iterations to see them. With
a fixed small thread count the 1 GiB limit stays a meaningful guard
and the 60-second timeout becomes a hang detector.

Check: a census of what each old test exercises against the gated
ones, each old test kept, split or retired with the reason; every
locale MT test passes in a few seconds under the harness in 15D on
any processor count, the 15D rows become a reference, and each test
still fails against the library before the fix it covers.

* TODO One atomic backend, the =__atomic= built-ins

The atomic operations have three backends besides the mutex
templates: the GNU =__sync= built-ins (=<rw/_atomic-sync.h>=), x86
assembly (=<rw/_atomic-x86.h>= over =src/x86/atomic.s=) and x86-64
assembly (=<rw/_atomic-x64.h>= over =src/x86_64/atomic.s=). Since the
per-width characterizations the =__sync= branch serves every build of
the matrix, 32-bit x86 included, where the former =__i486__= test
never matched a default =-m32= compile and every threaded build ran
the out-of-line assembly; the assembly branches are reached only by a
compiler whose =int= characterization fails. The =__sync= contract is
a full barrier for the arithmetic and an acquire barrier only for
=__sync_lock_test_and_set=, which =_RWSTD_ATOMIC_SWAP= inherits and x86
hides behind =xchg=. The library exports the assembly routines,
=__rw_atomic_add= and =__rw_atomic_xchg= at 8, 16, 32 and (x86-64) 64
bits, and binaries built for 32-bit x86 before the characterizations
call them.

Fix: one backend over the =__atomic= built-ins with explicit orders,
none weaker than today's contract: =__ATOMIC_SEQ_CST= for the
increments, the decrements and the exchange; weakening an order is
the next entry's business, site by site. The five characterizations
test the =__atomic= operations the backend uses, and the macros they
define stay. =<rw/_atomic-x86.h>=, =<rw/_atomic-x64.h>= and their
dispatch branches go. =src/atomic.s= and the two assembly sources stay
built and exported until the next minor version, which may break the
binary interface; then they go with their symbols.

Check: x86-64 and aarch64 suites unchanged against their tables; the
disassembly shows =lock xadd= and =xchg= on x86-64 and 32-bit x86, and
=ldaddal= and =swpal= behind the outline helpers on aarch64; the
exported symbols of the library unchanged.

* TODO Synchronization on atomic integer flags: a sweep

Several one-time initializations elect their initializer with an
atomic increment of an integer and publish completion through a
plain write to a =volatile= flag that the other threads spin on:
=src/locale_body.cpp:815= (the global locale: =ginit += 1000= after
=_C_manage=, readers wait on =ginit < 1000= and read =global= with
no acquire, and =global= is tested unlocked before the flag),
=include/rw/_mutex.h:286= (the static mutex, with a non-atomic
fallback where atomics are absent), =src/once.cpp:125= and
=src/iostream.cpp:247=. =volatile= orders neither the flag against
the data it guards nor the spin against the reads after it. The list
is a start, not a census.

Fix: a thorough sweep, later. Census every site that synchronizes
on an atomic or =volatile= integer, then decide each one: a release
store and acquire load through the compiler builtins, the site's
mutex, or =__rw_once=.

Check: the census in a note, each site with its decision; the
ThreadSanitizer build reports none of them.

* TODO =yi_US.CP1255= does not build on aarch64

=make locales= in a 15D build on aarch64 Linux stops at =yi_US.CP1255=:
=localedef= reports Error 308, "incomplete multibyte character in
character map file: expecting 4 bytes, found 0". No note records the
locales phase on x86-64, so whether it is aarch64-only is open.
Hypothesis: =char= is unsigned on aarch64 and signed on x86, and the
charmap reader depends on the sign of a byte.

Fix: to be found.

Check: =make locales= builds every locale in =etc/nls/gen_list= on
aarch64, and the generated databases match the x86-64 ones byte for
byte.

* TODO Keep instantiated facets until the program ends

A named facet dies with the last locale that holds it, and the next
locale of the same name constructs it again: the constructor, the
mapping of its database, the lazy initialization of its data. Until
the reference leak of racing =use_facet= calls was fixed, any race
kept the facet alive for the rest of the process, an accidental and
unbounded cache. In 15D on aarch64 the locale MT tests that construct
locales in a loop run slower with the leak fixed (5-run means, real
time, the atomic operations lock-free: =22.locale.ctype.mt= 11.9 s to
13.0 s, =22.locale.numpunct.mt= 15.1 s to 16.7 s,
=22.locale.globals.mt= 8.3 s to 9.3 s).

Fix: explore a facet repository that keeps every facet it
instantiates until the program terminates: what it costs in memory
and mappings, how the reference counts and =~__rw_locale= change,
and what it does to the lifetime of the facet data.

Check: to be designed with the fix; the timings above as the
baseline, and =22.locale.use_facet.mt= must still find no block
outliving its round, or be retired with the design.

* TODO The C++17 floor

The library is C++98 by design and characterizes the compiler rather
than assuming one. The intended step is to raise the implementation
floor to C++17: =nullptr=, =noexcept= in place of the dynamic
exception specifications the library still declares, =static_assert=,
=override=, inline variables for the facet statics, =<type_traits>=
for the hand-rolled traits, and the retirement of the
characterizations for compilers that no longer exist. This is
distinct from the conformance target, what the library provides to
its users, which is a roadmap of its own.

Not before the baseline; the =0= to =nullptr= sweep is one inert
commit on top of the floor change, never sprinkled in ahead of it.
Until then null is the literal =0=.

* TODO Strict ANSI as the rule, the additions outside the standard gone

=_RWSTD_STRICT_ANSI= (=include/rw/_config.h:234=, selected also by
=_RWSTD_NO_EXTENSIONS=) is an opt-in: it undefines =_RWSTD_LONG_LONG=
and defines 30 =_RWSTD_NO_EXT_*= macros, and the original README
calls the switch deprecated. The tree reads 38 distinct
=_RWSTD_NO_EXT_*= names in 40 files under =include= and =src=, eight
of which the strict block never sets, and the tests carry the same
gates; =tests/intro/17.extensions= exercises the additions directly.

Intent: strict conformance becomes the only configuration, and what
leaves the code tree is strictly what the library adds outside the
standard: members, classes, overloads and manipulator values that
ISO/IEC 14882:2003 does not specify, such as the =ios_base::failure=
subclasses, base-2 stream I/O and =ios_base::bin=, the
=stringbuf::str (const char*)= overload, the deep-copy
=basic_string::copy=, the extra =num_get=/=num_put=/=time_get=
members, the =void= =count= and =distance= forms, the =setbase= bases
beyond the required ones, the filebuf and stream-locking additions.
Everything the =_RWSTD_NO_EXT_*= gates cover that is not such an
addition is not part of the trim: an implementation choice the
standard permits (the in-place =assign= and =insert= of =deque= and
=vector=, the width kept on a failed insertion, the portable random
sequence, the primary-template definitions), a later defect
resolution the block disables under the extension name (LWG 559,
cv-qualified =numeric_limits=; LWG 568, the safe stream conversion),
and =long long=, which follows the C++17 floor entry. Those keep
their present behaviour and lose only the macro.

Fix, in order, one subsystem per commit with the suite tables as the
oracle: first the 38 gates sorted into the two lists above, recorded
in the commit that deletes =_RWSTD_STRICT_ANSI= and
=_RWSTD_NO_EXTENSIONS= from =_config.h=; then per addition, the code
path deleted with its macro, its test branches and its
=17.extensions= cases; per kept behaviour, the macro and its dead
branch deleted. The original README stays as it is, =README.md=
records the policy. Sequencing against the C++17 floor to decide
before the first commit.

Check: =git grep _RWSTD_NO_EXT_= empty under =include=, =src= and
=tests=; =17.extensions= reduced to nothing or retired; the suite
tables re-pinned in all six configurations with no row but the
extension rows moving; the examples build.

* TODO Leftovers beside the removed function probes

The removal of the C library function probes (f43be017..a44256a6)
kept to the code that read the probe macros. The census
for it found dead or stale code next to them. None of it is compiled
differently by any tracked configuration.

- =_RWSTD_NO_USING_LIBC_IN_STD= is defined nowhere. Fifteen headers
  test it around their =namespace std= blocks.
- =src/file.cpp= declares =mkstemp= and =fileno= under
  =#ifndef _RWSTD_NO_PURE_C_HEADERS=, which the file defines three
  lines above; the blocks cannot be compiled.
- =_cstdio.h= declares =gets= in =std= and =stdio.h= exports it, both
  in pure-headers mode only. The default mode leaves it out, as
  C++14 does.
- =_RWSTD_FMT_TYPE=, =_RWSTD_FMT_ASSGN=, =_STRTOLL= and =_STRTOULL=
  in =_defs.h= have had no user since the 2005 import.
  =_RWSTD_WCSFTIME_ARG3_T= has no reader outside its
  characterization. =config.h= carries =_RWSTD_NO_ABS_INT=; nothing
  reads it.
- =<cmath>='s =_FABSF= calls =fabsf= without the =::= of its 43
  siblings. The 44 =_<F>F= and =_<F>L= macros could be inlined into
  the overloads that call them.
- =21.cwchar= exercises none of =wcscoll=, =wcsncat=, =vswprintf=,
  =vwprintf=, =getwchar=, =putwchar=.
- =libc_decl.sh= only looks for headers now; its name says more.
  =headers.inc= holds one list, for one script.
- Hypothesis: =__rw_get_timepunct= calls =wcsftime= with wide literal
  formats whatever =_RWSTD_NO_WCSFTIME_WCHAR_T_FMAT= says; on a C
  library whose third parameter is =const char*= it would not
  compile. Check: =git log -L= on those calls for a platform note.

Fix: one commit per item, each inert on the six configurations
except the =21.cwchar= coverage, which adds assertions.

Check: the per-commit check of the removal (=config.h=, object code,
the non-debug recompile, the suite table) in =11S=.

* Documentation

Documents that describe the library as it is, for a reader rather
than for the record; they go under =doc/notes= with the =ref-=
prefix, beside the =analysis-= investigations. Each entry names the
document, what it must answer, and where the material already is.
None has a check beyond a reading; they are prose. Roughly in order
of payback.

** TODO A tour of the locale subsystem

Written: =doc/notes/ref-locale.md=, current as of
2026-09-27; the entry leaves after a reading.

Facets and their ids, the facet index scheme, =locale_body= and how
=combine= and the global locale share bodies, the byname facets and
the generated binary locale data they read. The MT race lives here
(=doc/notes/analysis-locale-mt-race.md=) and a reader chasing it needs
the map first. Shape: =ref-string-replace.md=.

** TODO Decision records

One short file per irreversible choice the revival made: the platform
retirement, the null-pointer literal, the commit shape, the Clang
configuration, the compatibility mutex in the string body. The public
tree has only the outcomes; the reasoning is in commit messages and
private notes. Material: the commit messages and
=doc/notes/plan-platform-state.md=.

** TODO A conformance record

Written: =doc/notes/ref-conformance.md=, current as of
2026-09-27; the entry leaves after a reading.

What of ISO/IEC 14882:2003 the library implements, where it knowingly
deviates, which LWG issues it applied (the headers cite them by
number), and which later library features it lacks. The roadmap the
C++17 floor entry defers to. The most valuable to an outside reader
and the most work.

** TODO A build-system reference

How the =BUILDTYPE= digits and letters map to flags, why =config.h= is
a function of the compiler and =makefile.in= only, how a
characterization is written and how its result gates code, the serial
phases. The original =README= covers 2008; the revival's rules are
spread over =README.md= and the commit messages.

** TODO The characterization catalogue

Written: =doc/notes/ref-characterizations.md=, current as of
2026-09-27; the entry leaves after a reading.

The probes under =etc/config/src=, each yielding a =_RWSTD_NO_*=
macro: a table of macro, what it detects, which supported compiler
trips it today, and whether it is still reachable. Shows at a glance
what the C++17 floor could retire.

** TODO An ABI and versioning note

What the shared library exports, how the version macros and the
=_RWSTD_VER= checks work, what changing the string body would break.
The 4.1.x compatibility clause that keeps the per-string mutex is a
binary-compatibility decision with no document behind it.

** TODO A test-suite guide

How the driver's =rw_assert= and =rw_test= work, the =TEST= table
idiom and its =bthrow= column, how the harness classifies rows, how
to write a regression against a table test. Fragments exist in
=doc/notes/ref-test-baseline.md=.

** TODO A threading model note

Written: =doc/notes/ref-threading.md=, current as of
2026-09-27; the entry leaves after a reading.

What the library promises under =_RWSTD_REENTRANT=, what the string's
reference counting does and does not protect, the facet caching the
ThreadSanitizer run named. The front matter for the MT entry.

** TODO A tour of the streams

The =basic_streambuf= buffering contract, the =ios_base= storage, how
=filebuf= maps onto the C library. Shape: =ref-string-replace.md=.
